\begin{aligned}
x &= \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta, \\
y &= \rho \sin \phi \sin \theta, \\
z &= \rho \cos \phi.
\end{aligned}
\] Plugging in $\rho = 2$, $\theta = \pi$, and $\phi = \frac{\pi}{4}$, we get: \[
\begin{aligned}
x &= 2 \sin \frac{\pi}{4} \cos \pi = 2 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \cdot (-1) = -\sqrt{2}, \\
y &= 2 \sin \frac{\pi}{4} \sin \pi = 2 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \cdot 0 = 0, \\
z &= 2 \cos \frac{\pi}{4} = 2 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = \sqrt{2}.
\end{aligned}
\] Therefore, the rectangular coordinates are $(-\sqrt{2}, 0, \sqrt{2})$. </think> <answer> $(-\sqrt{2}, 0, \sqrt{2})$ </answer>

